          "content": "### Faithful Summary for the Executor\n\nHere is a concise summary of key findings from three AI-in-healthcare papers on clinical NLP, ambient scribes, and LLM-based therapy:\n\n#### **1. Clinical Natural Language Processing (NLP)**\n- A study by Han et al. (2026) assessed GPT-4o mini's ability to summarize ICU notes from both clinician and patient (parent) perspectives.\n- Parents found the summaries clear and emotionally supportive, while clinicians rated them lower on accuracy and completeness.\n- The LLM summaries were more complex than desired and had issues with accuracy and hallucination.\n- Implication: AI summaries need to balance clinical fidelity with patient-centered readability.\n\n#### **2. Ambient Scribes in Clinical Settings**\n- Seth et al. (2024) outlined a four-stage development model for ambient scribes, from documentation automation to proactive decision support.\n- Benefits include reducing administrative tasks and improving clinical efficiency.\n- Challenges include privacy concerns, potential biases, and the need for patient consent and trust.\n- Implication: Ambient scribes have potential but require ethical and practical safeguards.\n\n#### **3. LLM-based Therapy in Mental Health**\n- Hodson and Williamson (2024) tested ChatGPT-4 and Google Bard on cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) tasks.\n- Both models could identify cognitive distortions, with ChatGPT-4 performing slightly better.\n- Bard excelled at reframing thoughts, but both models sometimes missed the core issues.\n- Implication: LLMs can support CBT as a complementary tool but should not replace human therapists.\n\n### **Common Themes**\n- **Dual Perspectives**: Both clinicians and patients must be considered when evaluating AI tools.\n- **Ethical and Privacy Concerns**: Data security, bias, and trust are recurring issues across all areas.\n- **Future Research**: More studies are needed on long-term efficacy, safety, and integration into clinical workflows.\n