          "destination": "Research_Docs",
          "content": "Adaptive robust optimization (ARO) allows decisions to adjust based on observed uncertainties, enhancing performance within the modeled uncertainty set. However, this adaptability can lead to overfitting and poor generalization outside the set. To manage this, ARO employs constraint-specific uncertainty sets\u2014larger, deterministic sets for hard constraints (e.g., non-negativity) and probabilistic sets for softer constraints (e.g., supply-demand balance). This approach balances adaptivity with robustness, regularizing the adaptive decision rules.\n\nConstraint handling in ARO is crucial due to the introduction of dependencies in adaptive policies. Deterministic guarantees are used for hard constraints, while probabilistic guarantees allow limited violations for softer ones. Robust counterparts of constraints act as regularization terms, stabilizing the solution.\n\nComputational results show that ARO improves solution quality within the modeled uncertainty set, as seen in a renewable generation model. Adaptive policies outperformed static robust solutions but at the risk of infeasibilities outside the set. Properly sized constraint-specific uncertainty sets helped maintain robustness while preserving adaptivity. Deterministic guarantees ensure robustness but are more conservative, while probabilistic guarantees offer flexibility at the cost of stability.\n\nARO is effective when uncertainty sets are carefully calibrated, balancing robustness and adaptivity. Future work should focus on principled uncertainty set calibration and integrating ARO with machine learning to enhance generalization."
